3. Iterative — Cleaner Stack Version

The same idea can be written using the common while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) pattern.

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> inorderTraversal(TreeNode root) {

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();

        TreeNode curr = root;

        while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {

            // Go completely left
            while (curr != null) {
                stack.push(curr);
                curr = curr.left;
            }

            // Process node
            curr = stack.pop();
            result.add(curr.val);

            // Move right
            curr = curr.right;
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Why this version is popular

Instead of:

while(true)

with an explicit break, we directly express the condition:

while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty())

The logic is exactly the same.

4. Morris Traversal — Most Space Efficient

Now we can improve the auxiliary space.

The recursive approach uses:

O(h)

stack space.

The iterative stack approach also uses:

O(h)

stack space.

Morris Traversal performs inorder traversal using:

O(1) extra space

It temporarily modifies the tree by creating links called threads.

Idea

For current node:

If there is no left child:
visit current
move right
If there is a left child:
Find the rightmost node of the left subtree.
If its right is null, connect it to current.
Move left.
If its right already points to current, remove the connection.
Visit current.
Move right.

Code
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> inorderTraversal(TreeNode root) {

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

        TreeNode curr = root;

        while (curr != null) {

            // No left subtree
            if (curr.left == null) {

                result.add(curr.val);
                curr = curr.right;

            } else {

                // Find inorder predecessor
                TreeNode predecessor = curr.left;

                while (predecessor.right != null &&
                       predecessor.right != curr) {
                    predecessor = predecessor.right;
                }

                // Create thread
                if (predecessor.right == null) {

                    predecessor.right = curr;
                    curr = curr.left;

                } 
                // Thread already exists
                else {

                    predecessor.right = null;

                    result.add(curr.val);
                    curr = curr.right;
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}

