Complexity Comparison
Approach	Time	Extra Space	Difficulty
Recursion	O(n)	O(h)	Easy
Iterative Stack	O(n)	O(h)	Easy
Cleaner Stack	O(n)	O(h)	Easy
Morris Traversal	O(n)	O(1)	Advanced

Where h is the height of the tree.

For a balanced tree:

h = O(log n)

For a completely skewed tree:

h = O(n)
Which One Should You Use in Interview?
If asked for the simplest solution

Use recursion:

Left → Root → Right
If specifically asked for iterative

Use Stack — your solution is excellent:

Go left → Push
No left → Pop → Visit → Go right
If asked for O(1) extra space

Use Morris Traversal.

Most Important Pattern to Remember

For Inorder Traversal:

LEFT → ROOT → RIGHT

Think:

while current exists OR stack isn't empty:

    go LEFT as much as possible

    POP
    VISIT

    go RIGHT

The iterative template to memorize is:

while (curr != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {

    while (curr != null) {
        stack.push(curr);
        curr = curr.left;
    }

    curr = stack.pop();
    result.add(curr.val);

    curr = curr.right;
}
Tree Traversal Cheat Sheet
Inorder   = Left → Root → Right
Preorder  = Root → Left → Right
Postorder = Left → Right → Root
Levelorder = BFS / Queue

Interview takeaway: For LeetCode 94, your iterative stack code is the standard optimal practical solution: O(n) time and O(h) auxiliary space. Morris is only needed when the interviewer specifically requires O(1) extra space.
